"Maps a rectangular table of numeric values to cell colors so patterns across rows and columns can be compared.\n\nUse when: A dense matrix needs pattern recognition across two discrete dimensions. Approximate magnitude and missingness matter more than precise numeric differences.\n\nAvoid when: A few values deserve direct position comparisons or color cannot express the needed precision.\n\nVisual grammar: Row and column positions identify the two table dimensions. Cell fill encodes a numeric value with a labeled scale.\n\nBuild: Define one value per row-column combination and retain missing cells. Choose meaningful row and column orders before drawing. Apply an appropriate sequential or diverging scale and optionally label only readable cells.\n\nCheck: Check values share compatible units or normalization is declared. Verify the color domain and midpoint match the numeric interpretation. Confirm missing cells use a style distinct from zero or the neutral midpoint.\n\nPitfalls: Rainbow colors introduce false boundaries and uneven perceptual steps. A table can imply adjacency relationships that arbitrary ordering does not support.\n\nExample: Show synthetic test scores across learners and subjects, with one shared 0–100 color scale and short outer labels.\n\nAlternatives: Clustered heatmap, Correlation matrix, Lasagna plot."
